# LF in the working tree, everywhere, whatever `core.autocrlf` says.
#
# Every tool that reads this repository runs on Linux: the CI runners, the toolbox
# container, the images. A Windows checkout with CRLF is therefore not a local variant of
# the same tree — it is a different input, and it has been wrong twice in ways that only a
# CI round trip could see.
#
#   * `mix credo --strict` reports a line-ending consistency finding for every file, which
#     is true of nobody else's copy — and a check looking for two consecutive newlines
#     matches nothing in a file whose blank lines carry a carriage return, so the real
#     finding is not buried by the noise, it is absent. Two of those failed CI while the
#     local run called the tree clean.
#   * A test that writes a shell script from a string literal writes the source file's line
#     endings into it. `dash` answers a redirection with a carriage return on the end with
#     "Bad fd number" and refuses to parse the file at all, so the script never runs and
#     the failure surfaces as whatever did not happen next.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git detect binary files and leave them alone. The index has always
# been LF, so this changes what lands in a working tree and nothing that is committed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are run by bash on every platform this project builds on — the Linux
# and macOS runners, the Windows runner's Git Bash, and a Linux container with the
# repository mounted from a Windows checkout. A `set -euo pipefail` with a carriage
# return on the end of it is not something bash accepts, and the error it gives
# ("invalid option name") says nothing about line endings.
#
# Redundant under the rule above and kept anyway: these are the files where getting it
# wrong is loudest, and a line naming them is a line somebody can grep for.
*.sh text eol=lf
install.sh text eol=lf
scripts/build-images text eol=lf
scripts/build-local text eol=lf
scripts/ci text eol=lf
scripts/credo text eol=lf
scripts/dev-down text eol=lf
scripts/dev-up text eol=lf
scripts/e2e text eol=lf
scripts/kind-down text eol=lf
scripts/kind-up text eol=lf
scripts/pitr-drill text eol=lf
scripts/remote-up text eol=lf
scripts/toolbox text eol=lf
dev/toolbox/entrypoint text eol=lf
